 "location": {
  "name": "Sossusvlei",
  "wiki": "Sossusvlei",
  "country": "Namibia",
  "region": "Namib-Naukluft National Park (Hardap Region)",
  "lat": -24.73,
  "lon": 15.34,
  "tagline": "Some of the tallest dunes on Earth, glowing red around a dead-end clay pan.",
  "intro": "Sossusvlei is a salt and clay pan in the southern Namib Desert, ringed by towering star dunes whose sand has rusted to deep orange and red. Nearby Deadvlei holds a grove of centuries-dead camel thorn trees standing on white clay, one of the most photographed scenes in Africa. The area sits inside the Namib Sand Sea, a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "UNESCO World Heritage",
    "value": "Namib Sand Sea, since 2013"
   },
   {
    "label": "Big Daddy dune",
    "value": "\u2248325 m tall"
   },
   {
    "label": "Sand sea area",
    "value": "\u22483.08 million ha"
   },
   {
    "label": "Desert rainfall",
    "value": "Under 10 mm a year"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "The name roughly means 'dead-end marsh': 'vlei' is Afrikaans for marsh and 'sossus' is Nama for 'no return,' because the Tsauchab River's water drains into the pan and never flows out.",
   "The dead camel thorn trees of Deadvlei are believed to have died 600\u2013700 years ago, yet they haven't rotted because the air is too dry for the wood to decompose.",
   "The dunes' orange-red color is iron oxide \u2014 the sand has literally rusted over time, so older dunes are redder.",
